{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "def find_kth(a, b, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the k-th smallest element from two sorted arrays using a recursive, index-based approach.\n    Raises IndexError if k is out of bounds.\n    \"\"\"\n    def _validate_k(arr1, arr2, k_val):\n        total_len = len(arr1) + len(arr2)\n        if not (1 <= k_val <= total_len):\n            raise IndexError(f\"k is out of bounds. k must be between 1 and {total_len} (inclusive).\")\n\n    def _kth_recursive(arr1, arr1_start, arr2, arr2_start, k_val):\n        # Remaining length of arr1\n        len1 = len(arr1) - arr1_start\n        # Remaining length of arr2\n        len2 = len(arr2) - arr2_start\n\n        # Base case 1: If all elements of arr1 are exhausted\n        if len1 == 0:\n            return arr2[arr2_start + k_val - 1]\n        # Base case 2: If all elements of arr2 are exhausted\n        if len2 == 0:\n            return arr1[arr1_start + k_val - 1]\n        # Base case 3: If k is 1, return the smaller of the current head elements\n        if k_val == 1:\n            return min(arr1[arr1_start], arr2[arr2_start])\n\n        # Calculate indices to check for comparison, aiming to divide k_val in half\n        idx1_check = arr1_start + k_val // 2 - 1\n        idx2_check = arr2_start + k_val // 2 - 1\n\n        # Get values at these indices, using float('inf') if index is out of bounds\n        val1 = arr1[idx1_check] if idx1_check < len(arr1) else float('inf')\n        val2 = arr2[idx2_check] if idx2_check < len(arr2) else float('inf')\n\n        if val1 <= val2:\n            # Elements up to val1 (including val1) are among the smaller elements.\n            # Discard these elements from arr1 and adjust k_val.\n            # The number of elements discarded is (idx1_check - arr1_start + 1).\n            # If idx1_check was effectively beyond the array (val1 was inf),\n            # it means we are discarding all remaining elements of arr1.\n            num_discarded1 = (idx1_check - arr1_start + 1) if idx1_check < len(arr1) else len1\n            return _kth_recursive(arr1, arr1_start + num_discarded1, arr2, arr2_start, k_val - num_discarded1)\n        else: # val2 < val1\n            # Elements up to val2 (including val2) are among the smaller elements.\n            # Discard these elements from arr2 and adjust k_val.\n            num_discarded2 = (idx2_check - arr2_start + 1) if idx2_check < len(arr2) else len2\n            return _kth_recursive(arr1, arr1_start, arr2, arr2_start + num_discarded2, k_val - num_discarded2)\n\n    _validate_k(a, b, k)\n    return _kth_recursive(a, 0, b, 0, k)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/598", "solution": "from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Number:\n    \"\"\"Holds intermediate values for an Armstrong number evaluation.\"\"\"\n    value: int\n    digits: list[int] = None\n    digitCount: int = None\n    sumOfPowers: int = None\n    isArmstrong: bool = None\n\n@dataclass\nclass Result:\n    \"\"\"Holds the final boolean result of an Armstrong number check.\"\"\"\n    isArmstrong: bool\n\nclass ArmstrongChecker:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates the algorithm steps for checking Armstrong numbers.\"\"\"\n\n    def compute_digits(self, n: int) -> list[int]:\n        \"\"\"Extracts the digits of a number into a list.\"\"\"\n        if n == 0:\n            return [0]\n        temp_n = abs(n)\n        digits = []\n        while temp_n > 0:\n            digits.append(temp_n % 10)\n            temp_n //= 10\n        return digits[::-1]\n\n    def count_digits(self, n: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Counts the number of digits in a given number.\"\"\"\n        if n == 0:\n            return 1\n        return len(str(abs(n)))\n\n    def sum_of_powers(self, digits: list[int], power: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the sum of each digit raised to the specified power.\"\"\"\n        return sum(d ** power for d in digits)\n\n    def armstrong_number(self, n: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Determines if a given number is an Armstrong number using internal dataclasses.\"\"\"\n        if n < 0:\n            return False\n        \n        number_state = Number(value=n)\n        \n        number_state.digits = self.compute_digits(n)\n        number_state.digitCount = self.count_digits(n)\n        number_state.sumOfPowers = self.sum_of_powers(number_state.digits, number_state.digitCount)\n        \n        number_state.isArmstrong = (number_state.sumOfPowers == n)\n        \n        result = Result(isArmstrong=number_state.isArmstrong)\n        return result.isArmstrong\n\ndef armstrong_number(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given number is an Armstrong number.\n    An Armstrong number is equal to the sum of its own digits, each raised to the power of the number of digits.\n    \"\"\"\n    checker = ArmstrongChecker()\n    return checker.armstrong_number(n)"}
